{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"reportable-commutual-rooster","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## The problem\n\n`rebar.llm.gate_admission.scratch_unavailable_detail` distinguishes three states and defends\nagainst only one:\n\n- declaration + proof -> mounted, proceed. Correct.\n- declaration, no proof -> volume vanished, REFUSE. Correct, and the case it was built for.\n- **no declaration -> assume this host has no dedicated volume, proceed on ROOT. Silent.**\n\nThe third state is not an oversight. The module says so explicitly:\n\n> ``None`` on a host with no declaration, so the guard is opt-in by PROVISIONING rather than by\n> rebar version — no laptop, CI runner or existing box changes behaviour.\n\nThat reasoning is sound and must be preserved. But it has a consequence nobody wrote down: **the\ndeclaration can only be written by the thing that also performs the mount** — `user_data.sh`,\nwhich cloud-init runs exactly once, at first boot. So a host that acquires a volume AFTER launch\nis *guaranteed* to sit in the third state: the volume exists in AWS, terraform believes it is\nprovisioned, the CloudWatch alarm exists, and nothing on the box knows it is supposed to be there.\n\nThat is exactly what happened on `dcc3-75ee-26ce-4840`: gate scratch ran on the root filesystem\nfor days with the fail-loud refusal INERT rather than merely unarmed.\n\n## What would fix it\n\nDerive the declaration from something that SURVIVES a post-launch volume attach, rather than from\na marker only the one-shot provisioning path can write. Candidates, none yet chosen:\n\n1. The committed expected-mount set (terraform's `var.gate_scratch_mount` is already the named\n   single source, and `tests/unit/test_gate_scratch_volume_aa40.py` pins its consumers).\n2. An explicit host-level config the provisioning path writes but a reconciler can also write.\n3. Querying the instance's own attached volumes from IMDS/EC2 — self-describing, but adds an AWS\n   dependency to a code path that currently has none, and must not fail closed on a laptop.\n\n## Why this is NOT folded into dcc3\n\nTwo reasons, both blocking:\n\n1. **Landing it while the volume is still unmounted would refuse EVERY gate on the box.** The\n   change must land AFTER `infra/runbooks/gate-scratch-mount.md` has been executed, so it is\n   sequenced behind dcc3 rather than part of it.\n2. It reopens a deliberate, documented design decision about opt-in-by-provisioning, and the\n   module's own docstring rejects the obvious implementation (\"a new `REBAR_*` literal or\n   `[snapshot]` key would be a new mechanism against the shrink-only ratchet\"). That is a design\n   question deserving its own review, not a rider on a bug fix.\n\n## A second, smaller residual\n\nThe expected mount set is still written per-volume in three places — `infra/terraform/user_data.sh`,\n`infra/scripts/check-mounts.sh` (hardcoded `/var/gerrit /var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch`), and\n`infra/scripts/observability.sh` (`DATA_MOUNT`, `GATE_SCRATCH_MOUNT`). A third volume added\ntomorrow therefore gets **no metric and no alarm** until someone writes them by hand. Making the\nset data, iterated by the periodic probe, would cover present and future volumes with one alarm.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] The \"no declaration\" state can no longer be reached by a host that terraform believes has a\n      dedicated scratch volume, without reintroducing behaviour changes for laptops or CI runners.\n- [ ] The chosen mechanism is justified against the ratchet, or avoids adding one.\n- [ ] Sequenced explicitly after dcc3's remediation, with the ordering hazard recorded.\n- [ ] The three-place duplication of the expected mount set is either resolved or explicitly\n      recorded as out of scope with its own ticket.","id":"1064-8c4a-f399-41de","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Gate admission's 'no declaration' state is unreachable-by-design for a volume attached after first boot, so the fail-loud refusal is inert"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788649764372338001,"uuid":"ad8c05c3-29e4-4a72-9a74-54d401fd8a3b"}